WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.200
With Colombia my friend Simone went in heavy
but Mexico is a real mess. Let's start with

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.740
the basics. Mexico, officially the United
Mexican States, is the 14th largest country

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.799
in the world and the 11th most populated,
with its 126.000.000 inhabitants. Politically,

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:23.231
it s a federal republic composed of 31 states,
plus the capital district "Mexico City". Located

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:27.740
in North America, the country is bordered
to the north by the United States, to the

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:33.149
south by Belize and Guatemala, to the east
by the Gulf of Mexico and the Caribbean Sea

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:37.760
and to the west by the Pacific Ocean. From
an economic point of view, looking at the

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.970
national GDP, we could consider Mexico the
16th largest economy in the world, but if

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:49.019
we dig deeper we find several negative socio-economic
indicators, such as high rates of poverty

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:53.750
and inequality that have increased during
the pandemic. And indeed, there are quite

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.649
a few Mexicans who claim that the country's
main problem is not drugs, but that this is

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:04.019
just a consequence of another omnipresent
factor: poverty. According to studies conducted

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:06.840
in 2020 by CONEVAL (National Council for the
Evaluation of Social Development Policy),

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.090
44% of the population, or over 55 (55.7) million
people lived in poverty. Despite the serious

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.940
lack of civil and human rights, including
one of the highest homicide rates in the world,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.880
Mexico's political system, strange as it may
seem, is that of a representative democracy.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.920
And it's maybe thanks to the process of liberalization
that the country experienced between the 1970s

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.390
and the new century that criminal groups have
found fertile ground for their illicit operations.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:39.790
Among them stand out the cartels that, as
the name suggests, are criminal associations

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:44.780
with the aim of promoting and controlling
the activities of drug trafficking but also

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:50.130
weapons, migrants and organs. Thanks to the
expansion of these groups, the weight of Mexico

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.880
in the criminal world has increased a lot.
Originally, the Institutional Revolutionary

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.380
Party (PRI), which ruled for 71 years (from
1929 to 2000), held a political monopoly in

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.080
an authoritarian system, shaping over time
what's known as the "perfect dictatorship".

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.349
The executive power was predominant over the
legislative and judicial. So a fake democratic

00:02:09.349 --> 00:02:14.560
system developed, based on non-competitive
elections, in which the state greatly reduced

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.760
civil liberties. In doing so, the party controlled
most of the economic and social sectors of

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.230
the country, denying the possibility of emergence
of social movements, ending up dictating the

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:30.700
discipline of illegal activities such as drug
trafficking. Over the years, social phenomena

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:37.019
such as urbanization, student and workers
movements, internal migration, industrialaization

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:42.090
and increased levels of education, however,
have opened a breach in the Mexican central

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.489
power, inaugurating in the second half of
the 20th century a difficult process of expansion

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:52.200
of political space. In addition, a series
of reforms of the electoral system (begun

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.770
in 1977) allowed other political forces, such
as the conservative right, the National Action

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.730
Party (PAN) and the Party of the Democratic
Revolution (PRD), full electoral competition

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:06.480
in the municipal, state and national spheres.
This democratic transition reached its peak

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.300
in 2000 with the presidential victory of Vicente
Fox Quesada. At the same time, the power structure

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.159
changed, with a clearer separation of state
powers and greater d centralization in favor

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:22.879
of local and municipal governments. However,
this drastic change has created new opportunities

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:27.970
for profit and investment for narcos. Access
to the free market and the elimination of

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:32.650
fare barriers, especially after the entry
into force of the North American Free Trade

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.579
Agreement (NAFTA) in 1994, tripled the level
of trade with the United States and opened

00:03:37.579 --> 00:03:43.030
new ways for trade in illegal goods. In addition,
the policies that followed eroded the Mexican

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:49.219
social fabric, greatly reducing the income
of about 50% of the Mexican population, which

00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:54.769
fell below the poverty level, pushing millions
of people, especially young people, to enter

00:03:54.769 --> 00:04:00.129
the illegal economy. Another deep-rooted problem
is the weakness, if not inexistence, of the

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:04.829
State in the internal areas of the country,
where successive governments have always been

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:09.669
unable to deal with the threat of criminal
organizations, preferring instead to join

00:04:09.669 --> 00:04:14.989
them, with the consequence that the most fragile
social categories, such as women, children,

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:19.840
indigenous peoples and migrants, pay the price.
The increase in the number of cases of enforced

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.550
disappearances, in which the state authorities
are often involved, contributes to a climate

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:30.259
of total mistrust of the police and justice
agencies, which appear unable to provide def

00:04:30.259 --> 00:04:35.911
nitive answers. Among the most easily corruptible
subjects are the municipal authorities, who

00:04:35.911 --> 00:04:40.580
often underpaid by the federal government,
are at the mercy of the criminal power of

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:44.379
cartels and organized gangs. For this reason,
in the media, for years the country has been

00:04:44.379 --> 00:04:50.699
defined with the term "narco-state" or "narco-democracy"
and this comes from the combination of narcos,

00:04:50.699 --> 00:04:56.280
political power and law enforcement. A dangerous
link that results in silence, impunity and

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.330
connivence of those who should protect Mexicans
in front of evident facts Think that in the

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:05.730
Global Ranking of Impunity 2020 - ndice Global
de Impunidad (IGI) - Mexico occupies 60th

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:11.270
place out of 69 countries analyzed, with a
rate of impunity 10 times higher than the

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:17.129
global average. Let's take a look at some
recent cases: in 2018 in Acapulco, all members

00:05:17.129 --> 00:05:22.440
of the local police department were arrested
for suspected ties to organized crime; and

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.770
in 2019 Genaro Garcia Luna, federal police
chief and then minister of public security,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:33.370
ended up in jail. Motivation? Turning a blind
eye to the movement of drug shipments in the

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:38.629
territory. In the rare cases when investigations
are opened, they always go slowly and end

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:43.639
up shedding light in the wrong places. But
to understand why drug trafficking finds such

00:05:43.639 --> 00:05:48.760
fertile ground in Mexico, we can look at a
geographical map: the country is strategically

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:53.199
located, representing an international hub
for illegal substances, natural resources,

00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:58.720
weapons and people, many of them from the
South American continent. Moreover, the particular

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:04.110
conformation of the territory, with mangroves,
huge deserts in the north, tropical forests

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:09.370
and large mountain ranges, including the eastern
and western Sierra Madre, allows criminal

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:14.569
groups to hide from the government army and
establish areas where they can freely cultivate

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:19.909
marijuana and opium poppy (from which, let's
remember, heroin is processed). Otherwise,

00:06:19.909 --> 00:06:24.750
labs are set up for the production of synthetic
drugs, such as fentanyl or methamphetamine,

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:30.099
both of which are in great demand in the United
States, a country that for years has been

00:06:30.099 --> 00:06:36.150
immersed in an epidemic of opium overdoses.
But Mexico's long coastline also facilitates

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:42.139
under-the-table operations, and port cities
such as Veracruz, L zaro C rdenas, or Manzanillo

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:47.270
have become central bastions for many groups,
facilitating the entry of drugs, weapons,

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:52.409
and drug processing chemicals into their ports.
It's likely that on many of the containers

00:06:52.409 --> 00:06:57.710
that regularly arrive there is a dragon printed,
certainly not in plain sight but with an undeniable

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:03.120
origin. Yes, because businessmen and traders
affiliated to the Chinese triads that send

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.570
the materials destined to be transformed into
synthetic drugs (such as ephedrine), in order

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:13.580
to evade controls in the country of origin,
have for some time moved their labs to India

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:18.629
and from there send the products by ship to
Mexico, where they are skilfully synthesized.

00:07:18.629 --> 00:07:23.879
Okay, but how does the United States influence
the Mexican criminal situation? We have a

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:29.240
fork in the road: the first road is the one
of the flourishing U.S. market and its endless

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:34.440
demand for narcotics; the second is the one
of the prohibitionist policy that the various

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:39.849
U.S. governments have tried, for at least
a century, to instill in the leaders of Mexico

00:07:39.849 --> 00:07:45.499
City. Over time, the so-called war on drugs
and Washington's interference in Mexican affairs

00:07:45.499 --> 00:07:50.389
has shown itself through unilateral actions
of border interdiction (such as Operation

00:07:50.389 --> 00:07:56.620
Intercept of 1969) and in the financing of
aerial fumigation campaigns of opium and marijuana

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:02.270
crops (see Operation Condor of 1975-76). The
attention of the White House towards Mexico

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:07.669
was also expressed by planning undercover
operations of U.S. federal agents, in particular

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:11.190
the DEA (Drug Enforcement Administration).
Since the 90s, however, security cooperation

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:16.949
has grown, culminating in 2008 with the entry
into force of the Iniciativa M rida, the most

00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:22.949
serious attempt to put on paper at a bilateral
level the fight against drug cartels. However,

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:27.660
if we are talking about trafficking, we must
mention another scourge: that of the illegal

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:33.110
traffic of migrants. According to the most
reliable estimates, every year at least 2

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:37.570
million people, including children traveling
alone in search of fortune, cross or try to

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:42.460
cross the border into the United States. That's
why, as the Los Angeles Times reports, nearly

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:49.390
25% of the 45 million immigrants in North
America are of Mexican origin. Also in this

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:53.880
case the activity is under the control of
criminal organizations in complicity with

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:59.410
Mexican law enforcement agencies. Over the
years, there have been cases where migrants

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:04.580
were arrested by police officers and then
sold to various criminal groups with the goal

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:09.420
of obtaining ransoms from their families.
And I leave you to imagine the end of those

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:15.470
who couldn't afford such sums. The numbers
speak of 84,000 desaparecidos two years ago,

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:20.020
some of whom disappeared in acid. With all
these data, it won't sound strange to you

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:25.070
to hear that Mexico is one of the most violent
countries in the world. According to Mexico's

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:30.750
National Institute of Statistics and Geography
(INEGI), as of 2018, the country has maintained

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:36.940
a rate of 29 murders per 100k inhabitants.
A rate higher even than countries that have

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:43.420
historically had a higher rate, such as El
Salvador, with its 20 homicides per 100k inhabitants

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:49.330
in 2020, or Colombia, with a rate of less
than 24. To give you just an idea of the magnitude,

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:53.700
even though one shouldn't compare countries
with such distant and different geographical

00:09:53.700 --> 00:10:01.279
contexts, in Italy in 2019, the homicide rate
was 0.53 per 100k inhabitants (ISTAT). However

00:10:01.279 --> 00:10:06.450
Among the most affected areas are the states
of Michoac n, Tamaulipas and Zacatecas where

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:11.690
clashes between gangs are almost the order
of the day. Often these clashes also end up

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:16.020
involving civilians, as happened during the
massacre of Ay tzinapa (26/09/2014), with

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:21.300
the disappearance of a bus carrying 43 students
to the city of Iguala: the boys were stopped

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:26.230
by the police, arrested on charges of wanting
to join a protest and finally handed over

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:31.140
to the Guerreros Unidos clan who killed them
and made the bodies disappear. Their murder

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:36.949
can be explained if we consider that the students
of Escu la Norm l Rur l are people of humble

00:10:36.949 --> 00:10:42.269
origins and actively interested in the socio-political
problems that afflict their land, to the point

00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:48.680
that in recent years hundreds of social fighters
have emerged from those classrooms. A criminalization

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:53.829
unfortunately remained unpunished due to the
obstructionism of many politicians, local

00:10:53.829 --> 00:10:58.829
authorities and journalists who spread false
information about the incident, preventing

00:10:58.829 --> 00:11:03.540
the truth from coming out. Or think of the
massacre of San Fernando, by the Cartel de

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:10.070
Los Zetas in the summer of 2010, where 72
of the 73 migrants bound for the United States

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:14.610
were kidnapped and brutally killed. The same
fate then happened to the judge in charge

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:19.959
of the investigation. And you may ask, has
justice been done? No, because it's worth

00:11:19.959 --> 00:11:25.899
repeating, federal institutions and drug traffickers
go hand in hand. Talking about violence in

00:11:25.899 --> 00:11:31.420
Mexico is also the same as talking about gender
violence: the data at our disposal speak for

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:37.589
themselves: according to a government report
in 2020, ? of Mexican women aged 15 years

00:11:37.589 --> 00:11:42.550
and older have experienced violence at least
once in their lives and also in the same year

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:47.930
feminicide has reached worrying heights, with
a record of over 3000 women killed (3723).

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:53.279
All this happens with almost complete silence
of the judicial authorities: evidence disappeared,

00:11:53.279 --> 00:11:59.339
forensic analysis that remain pending, abandoned
lines of investigation make it seriously difficult

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:04.170
to fight the problem of violence against women.
But now, in order to better understand this

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:09.709
whole situation of chaos, we should talk about
the real lords of the country: the drug cartels.

00:12:09.709 --> 00:12:14.480
Even an uninformed person knows that in the
90s the throne of the "king of cocaine" was

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:19.980
occupied by the infamous Pablo Escobar who
together with the rival cartel of Cali dominated

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:24.329
the international traffic of cocaine, but
in the last two decades, the supremacy of

00:12:24.329 --> 00:12:29.389
Mexican organizations in drug trafficking
has replaced the Colombian one, becoming the

00:12:29.389 --> 00:12:34.500
main regional threat. And where do they practice
their dirty operations, like smuggling tons

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:39.500
of drugs and thousands of illegal migrants?
Of course, the U.S. border! And they do it

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:45.110
using extreme and unconditional violence as
a instrument of terror and control of society.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:50.170
However, the border is not accidental, because
the big cartels are well aware that the dominance

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:55.630
of this area guarantees profits of millions
of dollars with which they constantly self-finance

00:12:55.630 --> 00:13:02.350
their activities. And how were these realities
born? In 2006, in Mexico, there were 7 cartels:

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:08.139
Mil nio, Sinaloa, Juarez, Tijuana, the Gulf
Cartel, La Familia Michoacana and the Pacific

00:13:08.139 --> 00:13:13.290
Cartel. Their birth was facilitated by the
proximity of the United States, the main global

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:18.480
economy as well as the largest consumer of
drugs. Simple law of supply and demand. Although

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:23.380
the production and consumption of substances
in Mexico - especially those derived from

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:28.270
the opium poppy and marijuana - date back
to the end of the 19th century, it was after

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:34.490
World War II that things began to take a worrying
turn. In the 1960s, drugs such as those mentioned

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:39.600
above began to be produced in the states of
Sinaloa, Durango, Chihuahua, Michoac n and

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.230
Guerrero, regions from which, particularly
Sinaloa, it was easy to creep into the States.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:50.290
One of the fundamental changes then occurred
in the 1980s, when Colombian narcos shifted

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:54.880
their coca routes from the Caribbean to Mexico,
helping the emergence of one of the first

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:59.699
major organizations, the Guadalajara Cartel,
led by Miguel ngel F lix Gallardo (nicknamed

00:13:59.699 --> 00:14:03.550
"Godfather," "El jefe de jefes/The boss of
bosses"). Gallardo was originally an agent

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:09.029
of the Mexican Federal Judicial Police. El
Jefe quickly became not only the first Mexican

00:14:09.029 --> 00:14:14.550
to act as a link with the Colombian cartels
in their heyday, but also the first to become

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:19.680
a point of reference for the Medellin Cartel.
This link allowed him to request a new form

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:24.230
of payment: if Gallardo was paid in cash for
the transport of cocaine from the Mexican

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:30.470
border to the States, from a certain moment
onwards he began to withhold 50% of the transported

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:35.510
product, a fact that allowed the narcos of
the Guadalajara Cartel to transform themselves

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:40.640
from transporters into distributors, thereby
greatly increasing their profits. Although

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:45.259
it was not the only organization present,
this cartel managed to gain a monopoly of

00:14:45.259 --> 00:14:50.440
the major marijuana and opium producing areas
as well as the main access points to the US

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:55.899
border, such as the states of Baja California
and Sonora. And how could he act undisturbed?

00:14:55.899 --> 00:15:02.230
Simple, in addition to his affiliates, politicians
and entire police departments protected him,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:08.690
remember? In 1985 Gallardo's thugs managed
to kidnap and kill Enrique Camarena, a DEA

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:13.160
agent who was investigating the organization's
trafficking. The incident provoked a more

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:17.610
active involvement of the U.S. agents that
ended with the arrest of the main leaders

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:23.029
of the cartel and the fragmentation of the
same. With Guadalajara out of the way in the

00:15:23.029 --> 00:15:28.670
1980s, three other large monopolistic groups
remained: the Tijuana, Ju rez and Sinaloa

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:33.870
Cartels. The competition between them triggered
a violent conflict from which the latter emerged

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:38.630
the winner, gaining control of the main routes.
Without forgetting its more international

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:43.769
dimension compared to its competitors, its
overwhelming power derived mainly from the

00:15:43.769 --> 00:15:48.920
cultivation of the "Golden Triangle", between
the states of Sinaloa, Durango and Chihuahua,

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:54.020
and its fiercest contrasts were with the rival
Juarez cartel, historically located in the

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:58.899
strategic border city of the same name, which
experienced its period of maximum splendor

00:15:58.899 --> 00:16:04.890
in the mid-1990s. Despite its strong connections
with local clan groups, the group ruled by

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:09.820
Amado Carrillo Fuentes (nicknamed "The Lord
of the Skies", due to the intensive use of

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:14.990
airplanes to bring drugs into the US), however,
ceded ground to the Sinaloa cartel, which

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:20.830
managed to wrest control of the border city
from him and confine him to the State of Chihuahua.

00:16:20.830 --> 00:16:25.860
For a time, Carrillo Fuentes managed to create
a "federation" of alliances and non-aggression

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:31.050
pacts to break off hostilities between most
of the rival factions but at the time of his

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:37.389
death in July 1997, many of the former partners
became independent and came back to fight

00:16:37.389 --> 00:16:43.069
for the spoils, leading to the dissolution
of the already fragile arrangement in 2004.

00:16:43.069 --> 00:16:47.510
What happens from that time to the present
day is another story but for my sanity and

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:52.180
yours as well, it's best that I end here.
If you have followed me up to this point,

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:55.760
I thank you for your attention and see you
in the next video. Ciao!

